No, that's why I chose the malgli form of {cpacu} to show initiation of the
job.

I'd be amazed if there weren't some quick easy way to express "finally"
given the absurd number of tenses, contours, intervals, etc... that we have
at our disposal.

....

and yet, I can't think of what it would be... It's something like, {mi se
jibri}, but I {co'a se jibri} and {pu ze'u na se jibri}.

Is it possible to say something like "mi pu citka .iku'i mi ca na citka"
without having to break it out into a new bridi?  Would "mi pu canai citka"
do what I want it to here?  If so, you could say {mi co'a puze'unai se
jibri} "I [start] & [past-long-time do not] have job".  Although I get the
feeling like I've done something very wrong here.

